( This is Growl )

( Growl is a concatenative, statically typed programming language, similar to
  Factor or Kitten. )

def ifte { choose call }

( Growl is statically typed, which means that type errors appear during
  compilation, and not during runtime. )

def bad { ( 1 "a" + ) } ( Try uncommenting that )

( Growl also has something I call "command syntax" )

( You type the word with a colon suffixed to it, however many expressions you
  want or need, and the word is executed at the location of the ending 
  semicolon. )

( This is useful for combinators that do control flow, as it allows them to feel
  more natural; notice how `ifte` is not a language construct, but a function. )

def fact {
  ifte: dup 2 <
    [drop 1]
    [dup 1 - fact *];
}

( Growl allows for mutually recursive words, too. )

def even { dup 0 = [drop true] [1 - odd] ifte }
def odd { even not }

def main {
  "Hello, world!"
  10 fact
  10 even
  10 odd
}
